GITHUB WORKFLOW

- Merge: Let's say you have created a branch for the purpose of developing a single feature. When you want to bring those changes back to master, you probably want merge.
Rebase:  A second scenario would be if you started doing some development and then another developer made an unrelated change. You probably want to pull and then rebase to base your changes from the current version from the repository.

How to keep it "Clean" in the future
To avoid that "spaghetti" look in your Git graph, try this "Clean Workflow":
- Stop using git pull (standard): Standard pull creates those messy merge bubbles. Use git pull --rebase instead. It keeps your history in a straight line.
- Commit larger chunks: Instead of five commits for "Content For Westminster," try to finish the whole component and make one solid commit.

The "Golden Rule" of Syncing:
- If you are on dev and want main updates: git rebase main.
- If you are on main and want to bring in dev: git merge dev.


Step    Action  Why?
1. Save     git checkout dev
2. Push Dev git push
3. Switch   git checkout main       Moves your local environment from the "workspace" to the "production" branch.
4. Update   git pull origin main    Ensures your local main is up to date with any changes others made.
5. Merge    git merge dev           This is the key: It pulls all the new code from dev into main.
6. Finish   git push origin main

